course: technology
lesson: 27
title: Road and railway safety
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A car, a bus or a train is a heavy machine that needs time and space to stop, and its driver can only stop for what they can see. Almost every road rule for children comes from those two facts: give machines room, and make sure you are seen.
explain: Think of the van parked outside school. You are shorter than its bonnet, so a driver coming along the road cannot see you behind it, and you cannot see the driver either. Stepping out there is like stepping out blindfolded.
explain: Cars that reverse are even harder: drivers looking back over a boot or out of a driveway cannot see low down, which is why car parks and driveways are not places to play.
explain: Railways add their own dangers: trains are fast and quiet, a second one can follow the first, and live rails and wires can kill without any train at all. The rules are short: never go on the line, never cross when lights flash, wait.
fact: Cars need time and space to stop, and drivers may not see you; at the kerb, stop, look and listen both ways, wait until it is clear, walk straight across, and use crossings where you can.
fact: Children can find it hard to judge how fast a car is coming, which is one more reason to cross at a crossing.
fact: Car doors can swing open suddenly; get out on the pavement side, and check for bikes and people before opening a door.
fact: Small children are lower than a car bonnet, so drivers cannot see them between parked cars; never step out between parked cars, find a clear place to cross.
fact: At a level crossing, never cross when the lights flash or the barriers are down; wait until they go up, because a second train may come, and a fast train can take more than a kilometre to stop.
fact: Never go onto railway tracks or climb on trains, wagons or railway bridges; overhead wires can carry 25,000 volts and the electricity can jump to someone close by. It is dangerous and also against the law.
fact: In a car park, hold an adult's hand and walk along the side, not behind cars; white lights at the back of a car mean it is about to reverse.
fact: Wait for the bus to stop before getting on, never run after a moving bus, and never cross right in front of a bus, because the driver cannot see small children close to the front; after getting off, wait until the bus has gone so you can see the road clearly before crossing.
fact: At night, bright or reflective clothes and a light help drivers see you far earlier than dark clothes do; that is why school bags have reflective strips.
fact: Cars back out of driveways where drivers cannot see low down; many accidents to very young children happen on home driveways, so driveways are not for playing.
fact: Headphones and phones hide the sound of cars, bikes and trains, and electric cars are especially quiet; take headphones off and put the phone away when crossing.
fact: A bus can be about 12 metres long and a family car about 4 metres.
exercise: Why should you cross at a proper crossing when there is one? | Drivers expect people there and it is hard to judge a car's speed | Crossings have a special floor that stops cars | It is only so that the lights get used | Cars are not allowed to drive over crossings at all
exercise: What should you do at the kerb before crossing? | Stop, look and listen both ways and wait until it is clear | Run across quickly before cars come | Look only the way the nearest car is coming, then step straight out into the road | Close your eyes and listen for engines
exercise: Which door should you get out of a car by? | The one on the pavement side | The one on the road side, nearest the traffic | Whichever is nearest the driver | The boot, so you are behind the car
exercise: What should you check before opening a car door? | That no bike or person is coming | That the radio is turned off | That the window is closed | That the seat belt is still done up
exercise: Why should you never step out between parked cars? | Drivers can't see you over the cars and you can't see the traffic | Parked cars might start up and drive off with you | It is rude to the car owners | The cars might set off their alarms
exercise: One train has just passed the level crossing but the lights are still flashing. What do you do? | Wait, because a second train may be coming | Cross quickly before the next one | Duck under the barrier carefully, keeping your head low and your bag on | Climb over if no train can be seen
exercise: Why is it dangerous to climb on top of a stopped train or wagon? | Overhead wires carry 25,000 volts that can jump to you | The roof is slippery when wet | The driver might drive off | The roof might dent and give way under your weight and you'd fall through
exercise: What do white lights at the back of a car mean? | The car is about to reverse | The car is parked for the night | The driver is pressing the brake pedal | The car is about to turn left
exercise: How should you walk through a car park? | Holding an adult's hand along the side, not behind cars | Running between the cars to get there fast | Walking behind the parked cars, where it's shady and out of the way | Down the middle where drivers can see you best
exercise: Why should you never cross right in front of a bus? | The driver cannot see small children close to the front | The bus doors might open on you | Buses always reverse a little after stopping to let people off at the kerb | Bus drivers don't follow the road rules
exercise: When should you get on a bus? | When it has stopped and the doors are open | When it is still moving slowly towards the stop | When it is just pulling away from the kerb | Whenever you can reach the door handle from the pavement
exercise: Why do school bags have reflective strips? | So drivers can see you much earlier in headlights at night | To make the bag look cool | To keep the bag dry in the rain, as the strips are made of plastic | To show which school you go to
exercise: Why is a driveway a dangerous place to play? | Drivers backing out cannot see low down | Driveways are slippery all year round, even in summer | Cars always drive very fast in driveways | Driveways are owned by the council, not by you
exercise: Why should you take your headphones off when crossing? | Music can hide the sound of cars, bikes and trains | Headphones break in the cold | Drivers don't like people with headphones and they won't stop for them | Headphones make you walk more slowly
exercise: Which cars are especially hard to hear coming? | Electric cars | Old diesel vans | Big sports cars | Noisy motorbikes
exercise: A bus is 12 metres long and a car is 4 metres. How many cars long is the bus? | 3 | 4 | 8 | 16
test: «stop moaning, i always cross behind the bus as soon as it stops, it's quicker» What is right? | Wait until the bus has gone and the road is clear to see | Fine, behind the bus is the safest place, the bus blocks the cars for you | Fine, if you run | Only dangerous in front of the bus
test: «shut up, I've got my headphones, I'll still hear a car» Why is that wrong? | Music hides the sound, and electric cars are very quiet | It's right, cars are always loud | It's right, headphones make you hear better because they make your ears work harder | It's wrong only for buses
test: You're late for school and the bus is pulling away from the stop. What is right? | Let it go and wait for the next; never run after a moving bus | Run and bang on the door | Run in front of it so the driver has to stop and let you on | Grab the back and hang on
test: «that goods train's stopped, dare ya to climb on the roof» What is right? | Never: overhead wires can kill without touching them, and it's illegal | Fine, a stopped train can't hurt you | Fine, if you are quick | Fine, if the train has no engine on
test: What would happen if a car came round the corner while you stepped out between two parked vans? | The driver might not see you in time to stop | The driver would see you through the vans | The car would stop instantly, cars always can | Nothing, cars never drive near parked vans
test: «it's 6pm in December and I'm wearing all black, nobody's gonna hit me on my own street» What is the smart choice? | Wear bright or reflective things and carry a light, so drivers see you early | Black is fine, cars have bright lights | Walk in the road so drivers see you better | Only bikes need to be seen, not people
test: In a cartoon, a boy plays football on the driveway and the car backs out and stops just in time because the driver saw him. What is wrong? | Drivers backing out often cannot see low down, so driveways are not for play | Nothing, drivers can always see behind | Cars cannot reverse out of driveways | The boy should play on the road instead, where drivers are looking out for children
test: You're walking to the car with Dad, and the car next to you shows white lights at the back. What does it mean? | It is about to reverse, so keep clear | It is broken down and waiting for help | It is about to drive forward out of the space | It has just been parked and switched off
test: Mum is parked on a busy street. You're in the back on the road side. What do you do? | Get out on the pavement side | Open your door quickly before cars come | Open your door and wave at cars to stop | Climb out of the window
test: «chill, the barriers are down but I can't see a train, we can nip across» What is right? | Never cross when barriers are down: fast trains come quickly and take a long way to stop | Fine, if you can't see a train | Fine, if you can't hear a train | Only cars must wait, not walkers
test: Five friends cross at the crossing, then two of them run back to fetch a ball. How many are still on the far pavement? | 3 | 5 | 2 | 7
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pedestrian ought not to emerge between stationary vehicles. | The pedestrian can neither be perceived by approaching drivers nor perceive the approaching traffic | Stationary vehicles are liable to depart without warning together with the pedestrian | The practice is discourteous towards the owners of the vehicles | The vehicles are thereby caused to emit audible alarms
exam: It is given that one train has passed a level crossing whereas the warning lights continue to flash. Select the appropriate course of action. | To remain behind the barrier until the lights are extinguished, since a further train may be approaching | To cross with all possible haste prior to the arrival of any further train | To pass beneath the barrier with caution | To cross provided that no train is visible
exam: Ascertain the significance of white lamps illuminated at the rear of a motor vehicle. | The vehicle is about to travel in reverse | The vehicle has been parked for the night | The driver is applying the brakes | The vehicle is about to turn to the left
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning electrified railway lines. | Overhead conductors may carry 25,000 volts, and the current may pass to a person who merely approaches them | Overhead conductors are harmless unless grasped with both hands | The current is discontinued whenever a train is stationary | The voltage is insufficient to injure a person
